"""Concept lessons in syllabus order. The ¶ marker separates teaching paragraphs."""
from teaching.foundations import put

put(9, '''A chat history, a payment ledger, and a thumbnail cache all store data, but losing a copy has different consequences. Before choosing technology, state what is accepted, when it is committed, how callers read it, and which failures it must survive. ¶ Identify the authoritative record and the recovery procedure. A database category does not establish its configured isolation, durability, or backup behaviour. Begin with access patterns and invariants, then compare implementations against them. This prevents a familiar product name from standing in for decisions about the actual service being built. || Identify authoritative records / Define commit and read contract / State tolerated failures / Test recovery and retention

A ledger can represent accounts and entries as related tables. Keys identify rows, foreign keys protect appropriate relationships, and transactions can group changes. SQL expresses operations on this relational model. ¶ Joins let queries combine related facts. Their cost depends on indexes, row counts, data distribution, and the chosen execution plan; a join is not inherently a scaling mistake. Normalised facts often provide a clear starting point for ownership and consistency. If a measured read path needs a repeated or precomputed representation, add it deliberately and state how it stays correct. The relational model and the physical query cost are related but different questions. || Accounts keyed by ID / Entries reference accounts / Transaction protects related changes / Query plan determines access cost

A user profile and its preferences can be stored as one nested document. A document database gives that unit an identity and often allows flexible structures. This can suit data usually read or changed together. ¶ Decide which structure is valid and how it evolves. Flexibility moves some checking into application conventions; it does not remove the schema. Also ask how large the document can grow and whether changes across documents need a shared transaction. Placing every chat message in one ever-growing user document mixes different growth and ownership needs. Design the document boundary around actual operations rather than convenient JSON syntax. || Profile document owns preferences / Validate supported document version / Bound nested collection growth / Cross-document rules need explicit guarantees

A session lookup already knows its identifier. A key-value store can retrieve the corresponding value directly, making this a natural access pattern. A key such as tenant:42:session:7 also makes the intended identity scope visible. ¶ Other queries, such as finding values by an arbitrary field, may need separately maintained structures. Check the selected store's read, write, expiry, and durability contract; key-value products do not all behave alike. A session's expiry and revocation policy must also be explicit. Retaining an old value after permissions change can be a security problem even when the key lookup itself is correct. || Tenant-scoped key / Direct value lookup / Expiration or replacement / Secondary query needs another access path

A conversation history is often read by conversation identifier and message order. A wide-column design can use the conversation as a partition key and a sequence as an ordering dimension, supporting bounded history reads. ¶ Work through the largest room as well as the typical one. One huge or popular partition can dominate traffic despite many storage nodes. Model the queries first, and duplicate representations for different queries only with a maintenance plan. State which joins or integrity rules the application must enforce. The common storage pattern is useful guidance; exact guarantees still depend on the implementation. || Partition: conversation ID / Cluster by message sequence / Read bounded sequence range / Hot conversation remains one pressure point

A fraud investigator may follow transfers from an account to devices, other accounts, and further transfers. When variable-depth relationships are central to the query, graph storage can make traversals convenient. ¶ A relational model can also represent edges, so compare actual operations rather than assuming relationships require a graph database. Bound traversal depth and fan-out: one high-degree node can create enormous work. Apply access checks during expansion, not only after collecting results. A traversal that crosses tenant boundaries can expose data even if its starting node was authorised. Query usefulness, cost, and permission rules must be designed together. || Start at account / Traverse permitted device edges / Bound depth and fan-out / Return evidence with authorization scope

A temperature measurement has a time, value, and dimensions such as device identity. Time-series storage can exploit nearby timestamps, compression, and time-based retention for these measurements. ¶ Choose dimensions carefully. A unique request identifier on every metric can create too many distinct series. Also decide how to handle late readings, clock errors, corrections, and downsampling. A device timestamp records what its clock reported; it does not automatically establish causal order. Preserve ingestion time or a sequence where investigation needs that distinction. Time-based organisation helps when it matches the measurements and queries, rather than simply because a row contains a date. || Device measurement timestamp / Ingest with source identity / Partition and retain by time / Downsample without hiding required detail

A search query needs matching and ranking rather than a transaction over the original record. An inverted index maps terms to document references and supports this retrieval path. It is commonly built from another authoritative store. ¶ If indexing is asynchronous, a new document may appear late and deleted content may remain in the index temporarily. Permission enforcement must remain correct during that lag. Keep source identity and version information so updates can be ordered and the index rebuilt. A search result is a derived serving representation; treating it as the only recoverable copy creates different and usually much harder ownership obligations. || Authoritative document version / Indexing transformation / Searchable postings and fields / Rebuild from authoritative source

An embedding converts content into a numerical vector. A vector index finds nearby vectors, often approximately, to retrieve candidate documents with similar representations. Its architecture must balance recall, memory, updates, distance calculations, and filtering. ¶ Preserve the source document and version that produced each embedding. The index is derived and needs a rebuild path. Approximate search can miss relevant items, and a similarity score establishes neither factual truth nor access permission. Apply the required tenant and resource checks to candidates. Choose this structure for a demonstrated similarity-search need, rather than as a replacement for authoritative records or exact constraints. || Source and embedding version / Candidate vector search / Metadata and permission filter / Fetch authoritative source evidence

Object storage offers named blobs and metadata. Block storage offers addressable blocks. File storage offers a filesystem namespace and operations on files. These interfaces suit different callers. ¶ A PDF upload often fits object storage with separate application metadata. A database engine may require a filesystem backed by suitable block storage. Check the actual protocol and implementation rather than assuming category-wide guarantees. An uploaded object and a database reference are also separate changes: either can exist without the other after a failure. Track incomplete uploads, orphaned objects, and deletion so the application knows when a file is actually usable. || Client uploads object bytes / Object identity and checksum stored / Metadata commits reference / Cleanup handles abandoned objects

Checkout performs short transactions over current orders. A revenue report may scan years of payments. OLTP describes the first kind of transactional workload; OLAP describes the second kind of analytical work. ¶ A large report on the checkout database can compete for memory and I/O. Copying or transforming data into an analytical store can reduce that interference, but adds lag, lineage, and reconciliation requirements. One engine may serve both uses adequately at a smaller scale. Measure their interaction before introducing another system, and decide how current analytical results need to be. The labels explain workload differences, not mandatory product assignments. || Operational transaction commits / Data pipeline exports changes / Analytical layout serves aggregates / Freshness is explicitly bounded or observed

To display one customer's full record, it is convenient to read fields stored together in a row. To sum revenue over millions of records, reading only an amount column can avoid unrelated fields and support compression. ¶ Row-oriented storage groups fields by record; column-oriented storage groups values by field. Each changes locality and the cost of reads and updates. Modern engines can combine techniques, so inspect the actual access path. Storage layout does not replace a sound logical model or useful indexes. Choose it according to which bytes the important queries need to read and how frequently those values change. || Row: fields for record A / Column: amounts across records / Point lookup versus aggregate scan / Choose by measured access pattern

A customer's current address belongs in their profile. A completed order may also keep the address used for that shipment. This repetition preserves a historical fact; changing the profile should not rewrite the old order. ¶ Normalisation reduces duplicated facts and update anomalies. Denormalisation deliberately repeats or derives data for a purpose, often a faster read. State which meaning a copy has. A cached author name might be a historical snapshot or a value that should follow future edits. Without that distinction, developers cannot know whether a difference is correct history or stale data. Every maintained copy needs an update and recovery rule. || One authoritative customer profile / Order retains historical address fact / Read model may duplicate display name / Each copy has an update policy

An order has several lines. Each line belongs to one order, refers to a product identity, and retains the purchase-time values needed for its history. An entity-relationship model makes those identities and cardinalities explicit. ¶ Show keys, optional and required links, and the constraints that protect them. Also consider lifecycle: deleting a product must not remove the financial meaning of an old order. A diagram is more useful when it states these rules rather than merely joining table names. Translate the relevant constraints into the database or application so they are actually enforced. || Customer owns many orders / Order owns many lines / Line references product identity / Historical values survive catalog changes

An order line should not point to an order that does not exist. A suitable foreign key can enforce that relationship in a relational database. This is referential integrity. ¶ Decide deletion behaviour deliberately: automatically deleting dependent records may be wrong for historical payments. An application check before insertion can also race with deletion unless an atomic mechanism protects the relationship. Across separate services, one local foreign key cannot enforce a remote record's lifetime. Use stable identifiers, explicit ownership contracts, and reconciliation appropriate to the business rule. The integrity requirement stays important even when its enforcement crosses different storage boundaries. || Insert child reference / Database checks parent constraint / Atomic commit preserves local relation / Remote relation needs a different protocol

Payments records whether a charge was accepted. Analytics may keep a copy for reports, but should not rewrite that financial outcome. Data ownership identifies who may define and change each fact. ¶ If several services directly edit one table, they share transaction and release assumptions even if their source repositories are separate. Provide controlled read and change contracts, with only the permissions each consumer needs. Ownership also includes schema changes, incident response, retention, and recovery. Naming a table owner is a start; the design must give that owner a way to preserve its rules while others use the data. || Payment service owns outcome / Consumers use documented contract / Derived stores preserve source version / Owner manages correction and deletion

List the operations before assigning an engine. Profiles need particular lookups and edits. Financial entries need transaction and audit rules. Chat history needs bounded conversation/time reads. PDFs need large-byte storage and lifecycle management. ¶ Several of these needs may be met by one system. A relational or document model could both serve a profile, depending on its actual constraints. Add another engine when its capability solves a demonstrated problem. Each additional store introduces backup, permissions, migration, monitoring, and expertise costs. Prefer a storage choice justified by the operations it supports over one chosen merely to include every database category in the diagram. || Define query and write shapes / State required invariants / Estimate working set and growth / Select the smallest sufficient storage mix

A document's lifecycle includes its original bytes, extracted fields, indexes, archives, and recoverable copies. Deleting only the live row may leave other copies usable. ¶ Define purpose, retention, expiration, archival, and deletion behaviour from the start. Dropping time partitions can efficiently expire old data; deleting one user's records needs identity-aware propagation. A minimal deletion marker may be needed to prevent old events from recreating removed data, but that marker can itself retain personal information. Keep only what its purpose needs. Test restoration from an old backup so required deletion decisions are reapplied instead of silently reversed. || Record purpose and expiry / Delete or archive authoritative data / Propagate to derived stores / Reapply deletion after recovery

A shop adds a search engine because its current queries cannot meet a demonstrated relevance need. It now uses different stores for different workloads: polyglot persistence. ¶ The extra capability comes with indexing lag, rebuilds, permissions, schema changes, and another recovery procedure. A change across two stores is not automatically one transaction. Identify which record is authoritative and which representation can be rebuilt, then monitor progress between them. Compare the complete operating cost with the benefit of the new query path. Keeping fewer engines can be an advantage when one already meets the requirements. || Add specialized read engine / Define source of truth / Maintain and reconcile derived state / Pay operating and recovery cost''')

put(10, '''A lookup by task identifier looks simple to its caller. Inside the database, the engine must choose a way to find the task, obtain the stored data, and decide which row version the caller may see. Following these steps helps us explain where the time goes. ¶ An index is a separate search structure that helps locate records by selected values. For a lookup using an index, the engine searches its entries and follows the resulting record reference. It may need to bring parts of both the index and the table from storage into memory. Other queries may scan the table instead. The planner chooses an access path: the sequence of scans, searches, and other operations used to obtain the result. ¶ Two queries can therefore wait for different reasons. One needs storage reads; another spends CPU time filtering records; a third waits for a lock held by another transaction. Inspect the plan and resource behaviour before adding servers. The useful change is the one that addresses the expensive step. || Query reaches engine / Planner selects access path / Pages and versions are read / Visible records become results

Many database engines organise storage into pages. A page is a unit of storage the engine reads and manages together; it can contain several records or index entries. A buffer pool keeps useful pages in memory so later operations can reuse them. ¶ Suppose an index identifies the page containing a task. If that page is already in the buffer pool, the engine can use its memory copy. Otherwise, it must obtain the page from lower storage layers, which may require device I/O. Even a memory hit still involves work, such as finding the record and checking its visibility. The operating system may also cache data, so a database buffer miss need not mean a physical device read. ¶ The relevant memory question is which actively used pages fit. A query that visits a million scattered pages can put heavy demand on storage even when it returns few records. Extra web servers may only send more of those reads. Examine the pages touched and their locality: whether related accesses reuse nearby or already cached data. Page layout and replacement rules vary by engine. || Record stored on page / Index locates candidate page / Buffer pool hit or disk read / Visibility check selects record version

Suppose a query asks for keys 20 through 35. An ordered index can locate the beginning of that range and continue through its entries, rather than checking every record. B-tree-family structures organise keys into a tree of pages, with separators that direct a search toward a smaller range. ¶ In the figure, the root has separators 20 and 40. Key 25 belongs to the middle range, [20,40), so the search follows that branch to the leaf containing 20, 25, and 30. B+tree designs commonly keep records or record references in leaves, and allow a range scan to continue through neighbouring leaves. Larger trees add internal pages between the root and leaves; each step narrows the range. ¶ A hash index instead uses a hash calculated from a key to find equality candidates. It does not provide the same ordered walk from 20 to 35. Choose an index using the query's conditions, ordering, engine support, and update cost. A short tree path can still require page reads, and a range returning many rows still takes time to read and deliver. || Root chooses key range / Internal page narrows interval / Leaf locates key 20 / Neighboring leaf supplies range results

An LSM tree, short for log-structured merge tree, organises updates by collecting them in memory and writing sorted groups to storage. Such a group is called a sorted run. Later, compaction merges runs and removes versions that the engine no longer needs. ¶ In the illustrated path, the engine first records the write in a recovery log under its durability policy, then updates the in-memory structure. When that structure is flushed, it becomes an immutable sorted run: later writes go elsewhere rather than editing that run in place. After several flushes, the same key may appear in several runs with different values. A read must find the relevant version; compaction combines this scattered history when permitted. ¶ Batching can turn many small changes into more sequential storage work. But compaction reads and writes existing data again. Write amplification is the physical bytes written divided by the bytes of logical changes. Its value depends on the layout and workload. Observe sustained ingestion and tail latency, because compaction can compete with requests for bandwidth or fall behind and cause stalls. A short burst that fits in memory conceals this later work. || Append durable intent / Update in-memory sorted state / Flush immutable sorted run / Compaction rewrites and merges runs

A SQL query describes the desired result. The planner chooses how to obtain it, using statistics about the data and estimates of the work required. EXPLAIN shows that selected plan. An execution-analysis form can also report actual work; it may run the statement, including its side effects. ¶ Suppose the query asks for yesterday's orders. An index search may be useful if few orders match. If most of the table matches, a scan may be cheaper than many separate record fetches. The estimate of how many rows will match is called a cardinality estimate, and it influences later choices such as how to join the result to another table. ¶ Now suppose the planner expects ten rows and the operation actually produces a million. A join strategy chosen for the small estimate may perform much more work than expected. Compare estimated and actual row counts, filters, sorts, scans, and buffer activity. Updated statistics or a query rewrite may address the cause. Check the engine's command behaviour before analysing a statement that changes data. || SQL expresses result / Statistics estimate cardinality / Planner chooses joins and scans / Compare actual rows with estimates

A tenant-scoped list query filters by tenant and sorts by time and identifier. An ordered index on (tenant_id,created_at,id) may support that access path, depending on the engine and query. ¶ The leading tenant field groups one tenant's entries. Within that group, the time and ID fields provide the requested order; the ID distinguishes records with the same time. A query that fixes the tenant can locate that group and walk a bounded matching range. If it filters or orders by different fields, the same index may fit less well. An index serves particular operations, rather than making every query on the table faster. ¶ Each index also consumes space and must be maintained when relevant data changes. A covering index contains the values needed for a query and may avoid some table-record fetches, subject to the engine's visibility rules. Its larger entries also use more memory and storage. Test representative values and distributions: a small uniform dataset can conceal a tenant holding most of the rows. Inspect both the resulting read plan and the additional write cost. || Query filters tenant / Index orders time and ID / Read bounded matching range / Writes maintain index entries too

Start with account A holding 100 and account B holding 50. A transfer of 10 should leave A=90 and B=60, preserving the total of 150. Saving only the debit would leave the two-account model short by 10. A transaction groups the related database changes into one unit. ¶ ACID names four properties of transactions. Atomicity means the related changes are accepted together or abandoned together. Consistency concerns preserving the required invariants: rules such as preserving this total. The application logic and database constraints must correctly express those rules. Isolation controls the observations and interactions allowed when transactions run concurrently. Durability concerns retaining committed changes under the configured guarantees and failure assumptions. ¶ These properties answer different questions. Atomicity can keep the debit and credit together, but it cannot recognise that the application calculated the wrong amount. Likewise, placing statements in a transaction does not tell us which isolation level they use. To judge the transfer, check its calculation, constraints, concurrency behaviour, and commit guarantee. || Begin transaction / Validate declared invariant / Apply related changes / Commit under isolation and durability contract

The application changes rows and asks the database to commit. Commit accepts the transaction under the engine's configured guarantees. If the application promises a saved result, it should report success only after the commit succeeds. A rollback abandons the transaction's uncommitted database changes. ¶ The database's decision and the client's receipt of that decision are separate events. Suppose the database commits, then the connection breaks before the reply reaches the application. The rows may already be saved even though the caller has no confirmation. A connection error at this point does not establish rollback. Recovery needs an operation identity stored with the change, or another reliable way to find its outcome before deciding whether to try again. ¶ The transaction boundary also limits what rollback can undo. If the application already charged a payment provider over HTTP, rolling back SQL does not reverse that charge. Replaying the operation without accounting for the earlier charge can repeat an external effect. Trace local changes, commit, and remote actions separately when designing recovery. || Update local transaction / Request commit / Connection may fail around result / Reconcile uncertain outcome by identity

A database may commit while modified data pages are still in memory, awaiting their final storage writes. Write-ahead logging, or WAL, makes this possible by recording recovery information before the corresponding changed pages are written to persistent storage. ¶ Consider a logged change to a task. The engine creates the log records and modifies the page in memory. Under a commit policy that requires durable local WAL, it flushes the required records, including the commit record, before acknowledging success. It can write the data page later. If a crash happens first, recovery uses the persisted log to redo the required work and restore the appropriate database state. Commit therefore need not wait for every modified table and index page to reach its final location. ¶ The exact acknowledgement guarantee depends on synchronization and replication settings. A checkpoint advances the work already reflected in stored pages and limits later recovery work; it is not a backup. If the only device holding both data and log is destroyed, this recovery path is lost too. Independent recovery copies address that different failure. || Change produces log record / Required log persistence precedes data / Commit point follows configured policy / Recovery replays appropriate logged work

Suppose a stored price changes from 10 to 12 while a reader is using it. Multiversion concurrency control, or MVCC, lets the engine retain multiple row versions and select the one the reader is allowed to see. A snapshot defines that reader's view according to the engine's visibility rules. ¶ In the figure, version one, containing 10, commits at logical time 10. A snapshot at 15 sees that version. Version two, containing 12, commits at 20, and a later snapshot at 25 sees it. The older snapshot can continue to use version one even after version two commits. These are illustrative logical times, rather than a requirement that an engine use wall-clock timestamps this way. ¶ Versions reduce some direct interference between readers and writers, but the isolation policy still decides when snapshots are taken and how conflicts are handled. Long readers can keep old versions needed and delay cleanup. Stable snapshots also do not by themselves prevent two transactions from making incompatible decisions about different rows. We examine that history in the next chapter. || Reader snapshot established / Writer creates new row version / Reader sees its allowed version / Cleanup waits for relevant readers

Two buyers read an available seat at version seven. Each proposes an update that changes the owner and advances the version, but only where version=7. One update succeeds and advances the version to eight. The other can no longer satisfy its version-seven condition. ¶ The condition and change must be one atomic database operation. Reading a version and then writing unconditionally in a separate step leaves time for another buyer to intervene. This guarded change is optimistic concurrency control: do the work on an expected state, then validate that expectation when applying it. For the illustrated competing claims, zero affected rows means the claim did not occur. The application must re-read and make a fresh business decision. Some isolation implementations may instead report a retryable conflict. ¶ Pessimistic locking coordinates access before conflicting work. A transaction might lock the seat, check availability, update it, and commit before releasing protection. Another claimant waits and then checks the resulting state. Locks can also deadlock: A holds row X while waiting for Y, and B holds Y while waiting for X. Consistent lock ordering helps avoid this cycle; the engine may abort a participant to resolve it. Choose the method for the complete invariant and expected contention. || Read seat version 7 / Compute proposed reservation / Conditional update expects version 7 / One winner advances version to 8

Different concurrent histories produce different read anomalies. For a dirty read, transaction A changes a value from 10 to 20 without committing; B reads 20; A then rolls back. B has used another transaction's uncommitted value, which never became committed state. ¶ For a nonrepeatable read, B first reads 10. A then changes the same row to 20 and commits. B reads it again and gets 20. Both values were committed when observed, but the repeated read did not stay stable. ¶ A phantom concerns the rows matching a condition. B asks for reservations for a flight and finds three. A inserts a fourth matching reservation and commits. B repeats the same query and finds four. A new matching row appeared, even though none of the original three had to change. ¶ These are histories to check against an isolation contract, rather than predictions for every database. For example, PostgreSQL 18 prevents dirty reads even when Read Uncommitted is requested, and its Repeatable Read also prevents phantoms. Other implementations can differ. Testing one repeated row read cannot establish all the guarantees needed for a query over a set of rows. || Read matching reservation set / Concurrent transaction changes data / Repeat row or predicate read / Allowed result depends on isolation

A lost update can arise when two callers calculate from the same old value. Both read a counter at 10. A calculates 11 and writes it; B also calculates 11 and writes it later. If both writes commit, the final value is 11, although two increments were intended. B's stale replacement lost A's increment. This history depends on the statement form and conflict rules; an atomic increment or a checked version can change the outcome. ¶ Write skew has a different shape. Two doctors are on call. Transaction A sees doctor B on call and turns A off. Transaction B sees the earlier state with A on call and turns B off. If both commit, nobody remains on call. They changed different rows, so neither needed to overwrite the other's write. Each decision depended on a read that the other transaction made obsolete. ¶ The on-call rule spans both rows. With a correct check in serial execution, the second doctor would see that the first had left and would stay on call. The concurrent outcome has no equivalent serial ordering; it is a serialization anomaly. Protect the full rule with suitable serializable transactions, coordinated locking, or a shared conflict point. Protecting only each doctor's own row is insufficient. || A sees B on call / B sees A on call / A turns itself off / B turns itself off: invariant fails

An isolation level states which interactions concurrent transactions may have. Its implementation is part of the application's contract, so name the engine and version when relying on a particular behaviour. ¶ In PostgreSQL 18, ordinary Read Committed SELECT statements each use a new snapshot. Another transaction can commit between two SELECTs, and the second can see that change. Repeatable Read uses a snapshot established by the first non-transaction-control statement and excludes later concurrent commits from that view. It prevents nonrepeatable and phantom reads, but still permits serialization anomalies such as suitable write-skew histories. A stable view and a serially explainable outcome are different requirements. ¶ PostgreSQL Serializable adds checks for dependencies that could prevent a serial equivalent. The database may reject a transaction with a serialization failure; Repeatable Read can also require retries after concurrent updates. Restart the whole failed transaction, including its reads and decisions, with bounded attempts. Repeating only the last write retains assumptions from the failed attempt. Make external effects safe under replay before placing them inside retrying application code. Other engines and statement forms need their own documented analysis. || Select documented engine and level / Construct anomaly history / Observe commit or conflict / Retry entire transaction when required

Serializable execution allows transactions to overlap while requiring their committed effects to match some execution of those transactions one at a time. It is a property of the permitted histories; it does not require the engine to run only one transaction at a time. ¶ Return to the doctors. If A runs first and leaves, B must see A off and stay. If B runs first, A must stay. An outcome with both off matches neither serial order. A serializable implementation must prevent that committed outcome, for example by rejecting a transaction that the application then restarts with fresh reads. ¶ Serializability alone does not add a real-time ordering requirement between separate callers. Linearizability describes operations as taking effect at a point between their invocation and reply, while respecting real-time precedence: an operation completed before another begins must come first. Strict serializability combines this real-time requirement with transaction serializability. State which property the application needs. Even a serial execution of a wrongly calculated transfer gives the wrong amount, and a local database guarantee does not automatically include an external payment call. || Transactions execute concurrently / Engine rejects incompatible history / Committed effect has serial equivalent / Real-time order is a separate condition

A connection pool keeps a bounded set of database connections available for reuse. A request borrows one, performs its database work, and releases it. When all connections are held, later requests must wait, time out, or be rejected according to the pool's policy. ¶ Assume ten connections, each held for an average of 100 ms, or 0.1 seconds, per operation. In a simplified fully busy model, each connection completes 1/0.1 = 10 operations per second. Ten connections then give 10/0.1 = 100 operations per second before other constraints and variability. This is a modelled service bound, not a measured database capacity. Time waiting to borrow a connection adds to the caller's latency. ¶ Holding a connection while waiting for an unrelated HTTP service occupies capacity that another request could use. Shorten that interval where the transaction permits it. Measure acquisition time, connection hold time, lock waits, and transactions left open without work. Increasing the pool size may add database contention instead of throughput. Include pool acquisition in the request deadline so queued work cannot wait indefinitely before its query even starts. || Borrow bounded connection / Execute short transaction / Release promptly / Observe acquisition and lock waits

Reserving a seat and inserting its local reservation record must agree. If the seat change commits but the record fails, the stored data no longer describes one valid outcome. Put both statements inside the same actual database transaction. ¶ Begin the transaction, conditionally claim the available seat or inventory, and check that the change succeeded. Then insert the matching reservation and commit. If insertion fails, roll back the seat change too. If the claim did not occur, the application must not insert a successful reservation. The shared commit boundary makes both local changes appear together or neither appear. ¶ Check how the database library manages that boundary. An object-relational mapper, or ORM, maps application objects to database records, but calling save on two objects does not establish that both calls share one transaction. They must use the same transaction owner and commit scope. Also release resources deliberately: a lazy result that reads records later may still need its connection. Across independent stores or remote services, local rollback cannot provide the same common boundary; choose an explicit coordination and recovery protocol. || Decrement available inventory / Insert reservation record / Commit both local changes / Failure rolls back the local unit

A backup job reports success. We still need to know whether its data can be decrypted, restored, and used by the application. A recovery procedure must include the compatible schema, keys, credentials, and any log history required to reach the desired state. ¶ Restore into a clean environment and open the recovered database through the application. For a booking service, find a committed reservation and check that its seat ownership agrees. Measure the full time until the service is usable, and identify how much recent history is missing. These checks give evidence about recovery time and recoverable data. A file in backup storage alone does not answer either question. ¶ Replication maintains live copies and may copy an accidental deletion to them. Backups retain recoverable history according to their retention policy. Point-in-time recovery can start from a suitable backup and replay the required logs to a selected point. State which acknowledged writes the procedure can recover after each failure, and keep suitable independent copies. Test that procedure, including the application checks, before relying on its recovery promise. || Capture consistent backup / Retain required recovery logs / Restore into clean environment / Verify data and recovery objectives''')

put(11, '''A product description is read thousands of times between edits. Reusing a stored response can avoid repeating the database lookup or calculation. This reuse is why caching can help. ¶ Assume a cache hit takes 1 ms, a miss takes 50 ms, and 90% of requests hit. The simple average service time is 0.9×1 + 0.1×50 = 5.9 ms, excluding queueing and other work. Compare this saving with memory, lookup, and maintenance costs. A result used only once may be a poor cache candidate. Hit ratio also does not establish tail latency: slow misses and queues can still dominate some users' waits. || Repeated product lookup / Hit returns cached representation / Miss reaches authoritative store / Benefit depends on reuse and miss cost

A user repeatedly opens the same product: that is temporal locality. Reading neighbouring records together illustrates spatial locality. A working set is the data actively needed during a relevant interval. ¶ A cache much smaller than that set may continually evict entries just before they are reused. A large scan can also displace useful entries under some policies. Measure bytes and object sizes as well as key counts, and distinguish frequently used data from the full historical collection. Preloading every object can fill memory with cold content. Admission rules decide what deserves a place before eviction decides what must leave. || Hot working set / Bounded cache memory / Admission and eviction decisions / Observe churn and useful hit rate

Two application instances each keep a price in memory. One has price 10 and another price 12. Their in-process caches provide fast access but create separate copies whose freshness needs coordination. ¶ A distributed cache provides a shared network-accessible layer, adding encoding, connection, and failure costs. Choose by reuse patterns, duplicated memory, and required behaviour when a copy is stale or unavailable. Ordinary cached stock counts should not become the sole authority for exclusive allocation. Placing the cache elsewhere changes its access and failure boundaries; it does not by itself resolve the application's consistency rule. || App A caches price 10 / Database commits price 12 / App B sees refreshed value / App A may remain stale

A page can be answered by a browser cache, a CDN, an application cache, or a database buffer pool. Each stores a different kind of copy. Database pages and a rendered account response also have different keys and update rules. ¶ Draw the layers actually used and specify freshness at each. Invalidating an application object does not clear a browser's response. A public edge cache with a poorly scoped key can expose personal data. When diagnosing a stale result, identify which layer answered and which version it held. “The cache was cleared” is incomplete unless it names the particular copy involved. || Browser representation / Shared edge representation / Application object copy / Database page and committed version

With cache-aside, application code loads from the source after a miss and populates the cache. Read-through places that loading behind the cache interface. Write-through includes backing-store work in the write path; write-behind delays it. Exact ordering varies by implementation. ¶ Trace failures explicitly. A volatile write-behind cache can lose acknowledged changes before backing persistence. Cache-aside also has a race: a reader fetches version one, a writer commits version two and invalidates, then the delayed reader inserts version one. Where freshness requires stronger behaviour, use an appropriate version or coordination protocol. A pattern name alone does not resolve these event sequences. || Cache miss reads old value / Writer commits new value / Writer invalidates cache / Delayed reader can repopulate old value

A cache receives a value that was already five minutes old and gives it a one-minute TTL. The copy may then remain six minutes behind its source. TTL describes residence after insertion, rather than necessarily the age of the fact. ¶ Expiration also differs from eviction due to memory pressure. Choose lifetimes using tolerated staleness and refresh behaviour, and consider client-held copies. Adding jitter can spread expirations that would otherwise happen together. For a correctness-critical action, revalidate against the authority when required. A short display-cache TTL is useful only if it matches the promise made about that display. || Value observed at source time T / Cached later at T plus delay / TTL begins at insertion / Source age includes both delay and TTL

A writer updates the source and deletes a cache entry. A delayed reader can still repopulate it from an earlier read, leaving an old value after the invalidation. Simply deleting after writing does not prevent every race. ¶ Versioned entries, conditional population, or source-version checks can support a stronger protocol. Observe delayed or repeated invalidation events and their progress. Choose freshness per operation. Showing an older price may be acceptable if checkout verifies the actual price; allocating the last item from an old count can oversell it. Invalidation must serve the business rule, rather than merely keep copies approximately similar. || Authoritative version increases / Invalidation or update is emitted / Cached copy compares version / Critical operation validates authoritative state

At capacity three, access A,B,C,A,D. Ideal LRU treats the second access to A as recent and evicts B. FIFO preserves insertion order and evicts A. LFU favours frequently used entries but also needs a tie-breaking rule. ¶ Real cache policies may approximate these ideals. Test the actual policy with representative object sizes and request patterns. Eviction decides what leaves; admission decides what enters. Neither can make an arbitrarily large working set fit into memory. A policy that performs well on one tiny fixture may behave poorly during scans or changing popularity, so measure the workload it is meant to serve. || Capacity: 3 entries / Access A, B, C, A / Insert D / LRU removes B; FIFO removes A

A single product becomes extremely popular. Even a large cache cluster can overload the one owner serving that key. Adding nodes mainly gives other keys more capacity unless the design also distributes this key's traffic. ¶ For reads, local copies, replicas, or coalescing repeated work may help, with an explicit freshness policy. For writes, determine whether the value can be split or combined safely. An aggregate counter may allow distributed increments; exclusive stock allocation needs a stronger rule. Identify whether the pressure is transfer volume, recomputation, object size, or serialised updates before selecting a remedy. || One product becomes viral / One cache owner receives most reads / Add suitable read copies or coalescing / Preserve authoritative allocation semantics

Many requests miss the same key and all recompute it: a cache stampede. Repeated requests for nonexistent keys are often called penetration. Broad expiry or cache loss can cause an avalanche of origin traffic. ¶ Use controls suited to the cause. Single-flight loading combines concurrent work for one key. Negative caching can remember a genuine absence. TTL jitter spreads expiry, while bounded refresh and origin admission protect the source. A refresh lock needs its own failure and expiry behaviour. Test cache loss: allowing every application instance to rebuild everything at once can overload the database that remains responsible for the real data. || Many readers miss at once / Duplicate origin queries accumulate / Coalesce refresh for one key / Bound total origin work during recovery

A client repeatedly asks for a product that does not exist. A short-lived “not found” entry can prevent repeated source lookups. This is negative caching. ¶ Distinguish absence from failure. A temporarily unreachable database has not established that the product is missing. Likewise, one user's denied access must not poison another user's cache entry. Scope keys correctly and use deliberate lifetimes. If the product is created just after its absence is cached, it may stay invisible until expiry or invalidation. The policy should state whether that delay is acceptable and how newly created records become visible. || Product P absent / Cache absence for bounded interval / Product P is created / Invalidate absence or wait defined TTL

A cached product description expires while its source is slow. Stale-while-revalidate can return an allowed older copy and refresh it in the background. The user avoids the immediate wait, and combined refresh work can protect the origin. ¶ Define separate ages for fresh data, permitted stale data, and unusable data. Repeated refresh failures should not silently extend stale serving forever. A description may tolerate this arrangement; an old payment reply cannot authorise a new charge. Make degradation visible where appropriate and preserve authoritative checks for operations whose correctness depends on current state. Faster responses have value only within the agreed freshness rule. || Value becomes stale / Reader receives permitted old copy / One background refresh runs / Hard age limit eventually forbids serving

New application instances start with empty caches. Warming loads likely useful entries before normal demand; prefetching predicts what will be needed soon. Both spend bandwidth and memory before a user actually asks. ¶ Load a measured working set gradually rather than scanning the entire database from every new instance. Check whether prefetched values are used and whether they evict more valuable entries. Staggering a rollout, sharing a cache, or limiting admission may solve a cold-start problem more simply. Stop obsolete speculative work. The useful question is how much future work was avoided, rather than how many entries were loaded. || New instance starts / Warm bounded hot set / Observe useful prefetch ratio / Avoid coordinated origin overload

A set can track identifiers, a sorted set can organise scores or timestamps, and a hash can group fields. Redis-like systems expose such structures with operations suited to particular tasks. ¶ Choose by the operations needed, then examine their atomic scope. One atomic command does not make an arbitrary sequence of commands atomic. A script or transaction may protect the local sequence, subject to the selected system's rules and key placement. Expiry, persistence, and replica behaviour remain separate decisions. A convenient list or stream is not automatically a durable queue with the required acknowledgment and recovery semantics. || Select structure for operation / Keep compound invariant atomic / Bound memory and expiry / Check persistence and cluster semantics

An origin shield collects edge misses before they reach the source, reducing duplicate origin work. A CDN places permitted representations near users. Both depend on correct keys and sharing rules. ¶ Decide how query parameters, headers, identities, and content versions affect the representation. A signed URL is a capability with a scope and lifetime, not permission to ignore private-data policy. Purge propagation follows the provider contract and may not remove copies already kept by clients. Immutable versioned URLs can simplify public asset updates. Trace every remaining copy when immediate revocation matters; proximity and bandwidth savings do not establish access correctness. || Client to nearby edge / Edge miss reaches shield / Shield miss reaches origin / Versioned content separates representations

Partitioning assigns different keys to different owners. Replication makes additional copies under a protocol. A distributed cache may use both, but failover can still lose, evict, or expose delayed entries. ¶ Specify whether the application falls back, serves permitted stale data, or rejects when the cache fails. Stable hashing can reduce key remapping during membership changes, while leaving hot-key traffic uneven. Keep source and cache recovery capacity explicit. The real peak may arrive when a cache cluster restarts and every missing value needs reconstruction. Test that scenario rather than sizing the origin only for a warm-cache hit ratio. || Key maps to partition / Partition may have replicas / Failover can expose misses or lag / Origin recovery budget remains bounded

A cache adds a lookup, encoding, memory use, and copy-maintenance work. If results are rarely reused, those costs can exceed the work saved. If its key omits authorisation scope, the cache can also serve the wrong user's result. ¶ Test with the cache empty and unavailable. A warm cache can hide insufficient source capacity until the day it disappears. Sometimes a better query or index removes the bottleneck with fewer freshness and recovery obligations. Keep caching where measured reuse and the agreed correctness policy justify it. Removing an unhelpful cache can simplify both performance and reasoning. || Measure saved origin work / Count invalidation and operating cost / Test cold and failed cache / Remove cache when it worsens the contract''')

put(12, '''Suppose our URL shortener receives 100 requests per second. Over time, the target rises to 100,000 requests per second. These numbers describe the work we want to support; they do not yet tell us how many machines to buy. We first need to find which resource limits the existing service while it meets its response-time and correctness requirements. ¶ One option is to give the current machine more CPU, memory, or other resources. This is vertical scaling. Another is to add machines and distribute suitable work among them. This is horizontal scaling. A machine or independently managed server in such an arrangement is often called a node. ¶ Consider a web application that spends most of its time performing independent calculations. More CPU on one node may let it perform more calculations at once. Several application nodes may also share that work, provided requests can reach them and each can obtain the state it needs. By contrast, if every request waits for the same database lock, adding application nodes mainly adds more callers waiting for that lock. A larger machine also cannot remove a rule that requires those updates to happen one at a time. ¶ Choose the change that addresses the measured limit, then measure completed useful work again. Include startup time, cost, and failure consequences in the comparison. A larger database machine can postpone the need to distribute data, while several application nodes can make replacement easier. Neither arrangement promises a proportional increase in throughput. After one limit is relieved, another resource may become the next constraint. || Identify saturated resource / Increase node capacity or add owners / Shared bottleneck may remain / Measure completed useful work again

A user signs in through application instance A. The next request reaches instance B. To serve it correctly, B must establish the same identity, check the current permissions, and find the required application data. If the only record of the user's session exists in A's memory, B cannot simply continue where A stopped. ¶ A stateless service avoids keeping required per-client conversation state only in a particular application instance between requests. For example, a request can carry a session identifier, and either instance can use that identifier to retrieve the session from its designated store. The application still uses state. The useful property is that serving the next request does not require returning to the same application process. ¶ A stateful service keeps some continuing context that affects how later work must be handled. Local sessions are one example. A worker that has accepted a job into memory may also hold work that a replacement cannot reconstruct. Moving the session to a shared store does not, by itself, preserve that accepted job. Each kind of required state needs an owner and a recovery procedure. ¶ Before freely distributing requests, check that every eligible instance has compatible configuration, can access the required state, and follows the same contracts. Also preserve the handling of repeated operations: if a reply disappears, a retry reaching B must not accidentally repeat an effect already performed by A. The word stateless describes a useful arrangement of state; the replacement behaviour still has to be established. || Request reaches instance A / Required state has external owner / Next request reaches instance B / B reconstructs context under contract

With several application instances available, someone must select the destination for each new unit of traffic. A load balancer performs this selection according to a policy. The policy should distribute work in a way that makes useful capacity available, rather than merely producing an even count of selections. ¶ Round-robin selects eligible backends in turn: A, then B, then C, then A again. It is simple and can work well when instances and requests have similar costs. Weighted selection gives a larger share to an instance expected to handle more work. For example, a higher-capacity instance can receive a greater proportion of new traffic. The weights need to reflect usable capacity under the actual workload. ¶ Least-connections uses the number of active connections as a clue to current load. This can help when some connections last much longer than others. It is less informative when connections carry very different amounts of work. Similarly, even request counts can hide a few expensive requests that keep one instance busy. Hash-based selection uses a key, such as a session identifier, to keep related traffic together; this can improve locality but can also concentrate a heavy user's traffic. ¶ The selection can happen in an intermediary, which is server-side balancing, or in a caller that knows the available destinations, which is client-side balancing. In either case, decide who discovers backends, excludes unhealthy targets, and performs retries. Observe per-instance queues and saturation to check whether the policy is working. Redistributing requests cannot increase the capacity of a database that all those instances already share. || Choose backend by policy / Observe actual work and queue / Avoid unhealthy or overloaded target / Rebalance without multiplying retries

An application process answers a ping, yet every real request fails because it cannot initialise the state needed to serve users. A different process has lost an optional recommendation service but can still display the requested page. Both cases show why a health check must answer a particular operating question. ¶ A liveness check asks whether the process should be restarted. A readiness check asks whether the instance should receive traffic under the service's chosen policy. These decisions need different evidence. A process that is still starting may be alive but not ready. An instance that can give an acceptable reduced response may remain ready even when an optional dependency is unavailable. ¶ Suppose every instance checks the same optional dependency and declares itself unready whenever that dependency fails. One shared outage now removes the whole application from routing, including responses it could still provide. Choose checks that reflect the required response, give the checks bounded waiting times, and decide in advance which reduced behaviour is acceptable. ¶ Failover directs work to another eligible instance after the current one is removed or lost. A health check helps decide when to change routing, but the replacement must also have access to valid state and enough capacity. New traffic can be redirected while existing connections or in-flight requests still need handling. Work held only in the failed process's memory does not reappear because a routing table changed. Recovering that work requires its own state and retry rules. || Probe readiness / Remove target when policy says unsafe / Route new traffic elsewhere / State recovery remains a separate obligation

Suppose a load balancer selects instance A for a user's first request. Instead of selecting freely on later requests, it continues sending that user's traffic to A. This is a sticky session, also called session affinity. The routing arrangement may use a session identifier or another agreed way to recognise related traffic. ¶ Affinity can help when A holds local session state. It can also be an optimisation when A has already cached data likely to be needed by that user. These uses have different consequences. Losing a disposable cache entry may only make the next request slower; losing the only session record may force the user to sign in again or interrupt the operation. ¶ Now let A fail or leave service during maintenance. Sending the user to B changes the destination, but B still needs a way to recover the required context. Decide whether the session is recoverable elsewhere, whether the client must reconnect, and what happens to in-flight work. Keeping the user attached to A during normal operation does not answer these failure questions. ¶ Sticky routing also limits balancing. A few heavy users assigned to A can overload it while B remains lightly used. Externally owned sessions can remove this requirement for replaceable web instances. Where affinity remains useful, state whether it protects required state or merely improves reuse, and define how assignment changes when an instance fails or drains. || Client assigned instance A / Later requests retain affinity / A fails or drains / Recover session or reconnect explicitly

A worker queue's oldest unfinished job is getting older, although CPU use remains moderate. The workers may be waiting for storage rather than computing. A rule that adds workers only when CPU is high will miss the growing delay that users experience. ¶ Autoscaling is a control process that changes the number or size of running instances in response to observed signals. Choose a signal that relates to the constrained work. CPU can be useful for computation. Queue age can expose delayed jobs. Connection use may reveal pressure in a workload that spends much of its time waiting. A request count is easier to interpret when requests require comparable work; a changing mix of cheap and expensive requests weakens that assumption. ¶ The controller cannot create ready capacity immediately. It observes the signal, requests new instances, waits for them to start, and may then wait for caches or other resources to warm. Traffic arriving during this interval still reaches the old capacity. Keep headroom and admission limits for that interval, rather than assuming that an instruction to scale has already increased throughput. ¶ Set minimum and maximum capacity and use stabilisation rules so short fluctuations do not repeatedly add and remove instances. The maximum should reflect cost and downstream limits as well as the application's own resources. Adding workers that all wait on one overloaded database can worsen the delay. After scaling, inspect completed jobs and their age to establish whether useful capacity actually increased. || Metric crosses threshold / Controller requests capacity / Instances start and warm / Admission limits protect the delay

Suppose a database primary accepts a new record and sends the change to a second database. Keeping additional copies of data under a defined protocol is replication. The primary is the current write authority in this example; the other copy is a replica. What the caller can rely on depends on which events must happen before the primary reports success. ¶ In asynchronous replication, the primary may reply before the replica receives the change. Consider the sequence: the primary commits, the caller receives success, and the primary becomes unavailable before the replica catches up. If that replica is made the new primary, the acknowledged record may be missing. The second copy exists, but its progress matters as much as its existence. ¶ A synchronous arrangement waits for a specified replica milestone before replying. That milestone might concern receipt or durable storage, according to the selected system's protocol; receiving a record and applying it for reads are different steps. State which copies must reach which milestone. Waiting for remote acknowledgment adds delay and may prevent completion when the required communication is unavailable. ¶ Replication can serve appropriate reads or help continue service after a failure. To assess those benefits, examine copy placement, lag, and the promotion procedure together. Two copies on one lost disk do little for a disk-loss requirement. Likewise, promoting a convenient but insufficiently current copy can violate the intended durability promise. The protocol and tolerated failures give meaning to the copy count. || Leader accepts write / Protocol determines required acknowledgments / Replica may lag / Promotion must honor the durability contract

Suppose one database owns every short-link record. We can divide the records into portions, each containing some of the keys. Such a portion is a partition. When different storage owners manage those portions, the arrangement is commonly called sharding. Unlike replication, which makes additional copies, sharding divides responsibility for different data. ¶ A partition key determines the placement. If records are assigned by tenant identifier, a request for one tenant's links may reach just one owner. A query across all tenants may have to contact several owners and combine their results. The same key that simplifies one query can therefore make another query more expensive. Check the important reads and writes before selecting it. ¶ Ownership also determines where rules can be enforced. A shard may enforce uniqueness among its own records, but that alone does not establish uniqueness across all shards. A change involving several owners may require coordination that a local transaction previously supplied. Joins, transactions, and globally unique values need explicit treatment when their scope crosses the new boundaries. ¶ Keep a way to route requests to the current owner and a procedure for moving data when ownership changes. A large tenant can still dominate a tenant-based shard, and movement must preserve accepted updates. Introduce sharding when a measured storage or write limit justifies these obligations. A large user count, without a workload and resource diagnosis, does not identify either the need to shard or the correct key. || Choose ownership key / Route records to partition / Query may cross owners / Rebalance with explicit movement protocol

Suppose events have increasing time keys. Under range partitioning, each owner holds an interval of those keys. A query for a particular day's events can then visit the relevant intervals. Retiring an old interval can also fit time-based retention. The arrangement preserves useful order between the key and its location. ¶ That same order can concentrate writes. If all new events belong to today's interval, most incoming traffic reaches its owner, even while owners of older intervals are idle. Dividing historical data evenly does not divide current ingestion evenly. Splitting the range helps only if the new traffic can actually fall into different resulting ranges. ¶ Hash partitioning first transforms a key with a hash function and uses that result to choose a partition. Different keys can then spread across owners even when their original values are adjacent. The cost is reduced locality for ordered queries: retrieving one time interval may require visiting several partitions and combining their results. This extra set of requests is often called query fan-out. ¶ A design can combine a time bucket with a spread dimension, assigning today's events to several owners. This distributes suitable writes but makes a read of today's data combine several portions. Neither range nor hash partitioning automatically divides requests for one extremely popular key. Compare write distribution, read locality, retention, and the measured skew together; evenly placed keys are only one part of the problem. || Range: related keys together / Hash: keys spread by function / Hot key remains hot / Query locality and write balance trade off

A simple placement rule hashes a key and takes the remainder after division by the number of owners. This is modulo routing. It is easy to calculate, but changing the owner count changes the divisor. Many keys can then map to a different owner, creating a large movement task when capacity is added or removed. ¶ Consistent hashing uses a placement scheme designed to keep much of the old assignment when membership changes. In a common ring arrangement, both keys and owner positions are placed on a circular ordered space. A key is assigned to the next eligible owner position around the ring. Adding a position takes responsibility for part of an existing interval, so keys elsewhere can retain their owners. An owner can have several virtual positions rather than one, giving placement more opportunities to spread key ranges. ¶ Rendezvous hashing reaches a similar goal through scores. For a given key, calculate a stable score for each eligible owner and select the owner with the highest score. When an owner is added, a key moves to it only if the new score beats its previous winner. When an owner is removed, keys assigned to other winners can retain their assignment. ¶ These schemes calculate placement; they do not transfer records or preserve updates during a transfer. Callers also need agreement about membership and hash behaviour. Changing the hash function can change the mapping and therefore requires migration planning. Finally, spreading key assignments does not guarantee equal traffic: one frequently accessed key still has one selected owner unless another mechanism distributes its work. || Stable owner set / Map key using ring or scores / Add owner and remap subset / Transfer data and preserve request routing

A partition is being moved from owner A to owner B while users continue changing its records. Copying the records once is insufficient. An update accepted by A after its record was copied would be missing from B. Rebalancing must account for both the copied starting state and the changes made during the copy. ¶ Begin with a defined snapshot, which represents the source at a known point. Copy that snapshot to B and capture the subsequent changes. Apply those changes to the destination until it has reached the point required for handover. Check that the records and relevant invariants agree; a destination that merely contains some copied data is not ready to own new writes. ¶ Next establish the ownership transition. A routing epoch is a version of the placement information that lets participants distinguish the old assignment from the new one. The handover procedure must say when B may accept writes and how a request using the old assignment is rejected or redirected. Without such coordination, A and B could both accept incompatible changes, or a caller could continue writing to a copy scheduled for removal. ¶ Copying and applying changes consume the same resources that serve users, so throttle the move to leave serving capacity. Retain the old copy until validation and recovery conditions permit removal. Define rollback and reconciliation for an interrupted handover. Safe rebalancing is complete when data and request routing agree about the current owner, rather than when the initial copy finishes. || Copy partition snapshot / Apply concurrent changes / Switch ownership epoch / Retire old copy after validation

A cluster's average load looks comfortable, yet one partition receives 70% of writes. That owner develops a queue while the other partitions remain mostly idle. This uneven distribution is skew. A partition carrying a disproportionate share of the work is a hot partition, and its delay can dominate the requests that depend on it. ¶ First identify what is uneven. One partition may contain more bytes, receive more requests, or perform more expensive operations per request. These require different remedies. Inspect traffic, bytes, resource use, and waiting by partition. A fleet average hides the relationship between the affected requests and their particular owner. ¶ If the hot partition contains many independently writable keys, splitting its range or changing placement may divide the work. If almost all requests change one entity, creating more cold partitions does not help that entity. Read copies may distribute allowed reads, and batching may reduce some write overhead. A write rule that needs one authoritative decision still needs a way to make that decision correctly. ¶ Spreading append operations with an extra key dimension can help when the records are independent, but later reads may have to combine several partitions. Before choosing that trade-off, ask whether the pressure comes from data volume, popularity, or a rule requiring updates to occur one at a time. The useful remedy changes the specific pressure that caused the hot partition. || Fleet average appears healthy / One partition receives 70% of writes / Its queue determines tail latency / Split or redesign the specific hot path

A user changes a profile through the primary database and receives success. The next page reads a replica that has not yet applied the change. The old profile appears. Both requests may have completed as their databases allowed, yet the sequence fails the user's expectation of seeing their own update. ¶ Read replicas can distribute suitable queries. Read/write splitting sends writes to their authority and sends selected reads to other copies. To choose those reads, define the required freshness. A public report may permit delayed data, while the profile page may require read-your-writes: the next relevant read must include the user's completed update. ¶ One approach is to route the critical read to the primary under the database's required read semantics. Another, where supported, is to record the write's progress marker and use a replica only after it has applied at least that far. If no suitable replica is ready, the policy needs to say whether to wait, use another destination, or report that it cannot meet the requirement. The routing decision follows the observation the operation needs. ¶ SQL syntax alone cannot establish that requirement. A SELECT may acquire a lock or belong to a transaction whose decisions depend on current state. Splitting such a transaction across unrelated copies can change its meaning. Read replicas add capacity for appropriate reads; they do not make all reads interchangeable or remove the separate authority for writes. || Write commits at primary / Replica has older log position / Replica read can be stale / Critical read uses sufficient freshness

Replication usually has several stages. The source accepts a change, the change travels to a replica, and the replica applies it so reads can observe it. A replica can therefore have received a record without yet making its effect visible. Replication lag describes how far progress at one of these stages trails the source, and the stage being measured must be named. ¶ Suppose the source has accepted changes through position 100 in an ordered change history, while a replica has applied only through position 90. Its applied state is behind the source. Later it reaches position 95. In the trace, the source is still at 100 and the replica at 95, so a read there may miss the effects at positions 96–100. This is a comparison of progress markers, rather than proof that each position represents one second or one user request. ¶ Where the database supports them, examine source, receipt, and apply progress separately. A delayed transport and a replica that receives changes quickly but applies them slowly need different investigation. Time-based estimates also require interpretation: variable traffic means that a given position gap does not imply a fixed duration. An average across replicas can hide one that has stopped progressing. ¶ Use the relevant progress in operating decisions. A read-your-writes request needs a copy that includes its required change, not merely a reassuring fleet average. A promotion decision must honour the acknowledgment and possible-data-loss contract. Choosing a replacement quickly is useful only if its actual state is acceptable for the work it will take over. || Source commits position 100 / Replica applied position 95 / Read may miss positions 96–100 / Promotion and routing inspect progress

Several application instances need database connections, and database destinations may change during failover. A database proxy sits between clients and database servers. Depending on its features, it can choose a destination, manage backend connections, or let several client sessions share a smaller set of database connections. ¶ Sharing requires care because a connection can carry state as well as requests. A session variable, a temporary table, or an open transaction may belong to one particular backend session. If a later operation is sent to a different backend without preserving the required context, the application may observe different behaviour even though its SQL text is unchanged. ¶ A proxy may keep a client attached to one backend while that state is needed. This attachment is often called pinning. For example, the transaction must remain on the session that owns it until the transaction ends. Check which features the proxy can safely share and which require pinning, and define when the backend can be released for another caller. ¶ The proxy itself also needs capacity, identity handling, routing, and failure procedures. Reducing connection count does not remove transaction contention inside the database. Nor can a connection switch always repair a failed operation: a write may have committed before its reply was lost. Repeating that write requires the application's operation and retry contract, rather than an assumption that a fresh connection means nothing happened. || Client borrows proxy connection / Proxy selects backend session / Transaction pins required state / Release only when session contract allows

An application uses a pool of database connections so requests can borrow and reuse them. When every connection is in use, another request must wait or fail according to the pool's policy. Pool saturation means this borrowing capacity has been exhausted; it does not, by itself, explain why connections remain busy. ¶ Suppose ten application instances each permit twenty database connections. Together they can demand 10 × 20 = 200 connections. Add ten more application instances with the same configuration, and potential demand becomes 20 × 20 = 400 connections. The shared database has not gained capacity merely because more pools now point to it. ¶ There can be waiting at several boundaries. A request first waits to acquire a pool connection. After acquisition, its query may wait for a database lock while still holding that connection. CPU can remain modest throughout the lock wait, even as other callers queue for connections. Measure acquisition time, connection hold time, query time, and contention to locate the cause. ¶ Set an aggregate connection budget across the expected instance count, including capacity needed for recovery and administration. Include acquisition waiting in request deadlines and bound the number of waiting callers. Shorten unnecessary ownership of a connection where possible. Raising every pool limit can transfer a queue into an already constrained database; the useful goal is more timely completed work, rather than more simultaneous borrowers. || App instances multiply pools / Database connection budget is fixed / Borrowers wait and time out / Bound aggregate pressure and shorten ownership

A request takes a long time to complete. Adding web instances seems plausible, but most of the delay may occur after the application has sent a database query. To choose an effective change, follow the request through the stages that determine when its response can be returned. This sequence of dependent work is its critical path. ¶ Separate execution from waiting at each stage. The request may wait for a worker, run application code, wait for a connection, execute a query, and wait for another service. The stage holding up the required outcome is the bottleneck for that workload and target. Its cause may be insufficient processing capacity, a slow access path, lock contention, or admission of more work than the stage can handle. ¶ Compare offered traffic with useful completions, queue growth, and resource evidence. Inspect CPU, memory, disk, network, connections, and locks where relevant. High utilisation helps form a hypothesis, but does not establish which change will improve the user's result. A busy resource serving unrelated background work may have a different effect from the query every user must await. ¶ If one query dominates the path, a justified index or query change may help more than extra web instances. If independent CPU work dominates, added compute may help. Change a material factor, then measure useful throughput and the slower end of the latency distribution again. This checks the proposed explanation and reveals whether the limit has moved to another stage. || Trace request critical path / Inspect resource and queue evidence / Change demonstrated limiting stage / Re-measure useful throughput and tails

A queue holds work until a worker can process it. During a brief burst, requests may arrive faster than workers complete them. If arrivals later fall below completion capacity, the workers can drain the backlog. The queue has moved some work into a later interval; it has not increased the workers' processing rate. ¶ If arrivals remain above completions, the backlog continues to grow. Waiting consumes memory or storage and also consumes the caller's time. A request can become useless before the queue runs out of space. For example, a request with a two-second deadline cannot finish on time if it is silently accepted behind ten seconds of required work. ¶ Bound both the amount of waiting work and its age. Admission control decides whether to accept new work when capacity is limited. It can reject work that cannot meet its deadline, shed lower-priority work under the agreed policy, or reserve capacity for required operations. These decisions should happen before waiting callers consume the resources needed to complete existing work. ¶ Make the overload response clear enough for callers to react sensibly. Immediate repeated retries can add work to the very queue that caused the failure. Asynchronous acceptance is also a distinct contract: if the system promises to finish later, it needs to preserve and track that accepted work. A queue is useful for controlled waiting within a capacity and deadline policy; sustained excess demand still requires less admitted work or more useful completion capacity. || Arrival exceeds service rate / Waiting time grows / Deadline usefulness expires / Bound admission before resources exhaust

Two application containers run on one host. If one process crashes, the other may continue. If the host fails, both disappear. Counting two application boxes therefore answers a process-failure question, but does not establish that service survives a machine failure. ¶ A single point of failure is a dependency whose loss defeats the required service. It may be a machine, a router, a credential source, or a procedure that only one unavailable operator can perform. The requirement matters: a component can be dispensable for an already running request yet essential for replacing instances after an outage. ¶ A failure domain groups resources that can be lost together through a common cause. Place redundant capacity across the failure domains the service must tolerate, then inspect what remains shared. Two instances on different hosts can still depend on one unavailable state owner. Redundancy needs valid state, failure detection, a workable handover, and enough surviving capacity, as well as separated processes. ¶ Trace recovery as carefully as normal serving. Suppose existing instances can run with cached configuration, but every new instance must contact one configuration service during startup. Losing that service may leave present traffic working while preventing all replacement capacity. Include these startup and recovery dependencies when looking for single points of failure. The useful question is which required journey stops when a dependency disappears. || Two replicas share one host / Host fails / Both replicas disappear / Separate correlated failure domains deliberately

A service in one region is considering a second region to reduce distance to users or continue through a regional failure. Before choosing a topology, state the tolerated interruption, possible data loss, and location requirements. Then decide where each kind of write is authoritative and which copies can serve each kind of read. ¶ In an active-passive arrangement, one side normally owns the authoritative activity and another is prepared to take over. Promotion needs a rule for deciding when the old authority has stopped and whether the replacement has sufficiently current state. Recovery must also say how the former primary returns without resuming an incompatible role. ¶ In an active-active arrangement, more than one region handles normal activity. This still requires precise ownership. Some operations may have separate regional owners; others may coordinate before accepting an update or accept concurrent changes under a defined conflict policy. During a communication partition, the regions need a rule for which operations can continue and what each caller may observe. ¶ Waiting for a required remote acknowledgment adds propagation time to the write path. Asynchronous copying can avoid that immediate wait, but a promoted copy may lack recent acknowledged changes if the source is lost. Apply the chosen trade-off to actual operations: a delayed public page and an exclusive reservation need different contracts. A second region is useful when its authority, freshness, and recovery behaviour meet the requirement, rather than merely because another copy exists far away. || Select regional write ownership / Define acknowledgment across regions / Partition changes allowed behavior / Failover preserves stated recovery contract

Many users can read copies of a product description if the service permits a freshness delay. Two callers cannot both receive an exclusive claim to the last seat. Reads and writes place different demands on a design because a read observes a representation, while a write changes authoritative state under the application's rules. ¶ For suitable reads, replicas or caches let several owners serve reusable representations. The design still needs freshness, permission, and invalidation rules. Those copies can reduce pressure on the source, but serving more copies does not decide who wins a conflicting state change. ¶ To scale writes, first identify what must be coordinated. Independent tenants or records may be assigned to different owners. Batching can combine suitable work and reduce repeated overhead. Removing unnecessary coordination or maintaining fewer unnecessary indexes may also reduce the cost of each write. Each change must preserve the invariants the original operation enforced. ¶ One hot account or a global sequential allocator can remain a serial point even when thousands of unrelated keys are distributed. Some counter updates can be combined because their effects commute: applying them in different orders gives the same combined value. An exclusive seat claim has a different rule, because incompatible winners cannot both be accepted. The write design must distribute or reduce work without losing that rule; adding read replicas alone cannot supply it. || Reads reuse representations / Writes alter authoritative state / Partition or batch suitable writes / Preserve the actual invariant boundary

Suppose two candidate designs meet the same workload target: one uses a larger node, and another uses ten smaller nodes. The smaller machine's individual price does not tell us which arrangement costs less. Compare the complete systems at equal response-time, correctness, and failure requirements. ¶ Each node can repeat baseline memory, connection pools, monitoring, and operating work. Distribution can add transfer and coordination. On the other hand, added nodes may let the service add capacity in smaller steps or keep useful capacity through a failure. Count both the added work and the benefit under the actual workload, rather than assuming that scale-out is automatically cheaper or more expensive. ¶ Include spare capacity for the same failures in both candidates. Also account for startup, cache warming, migration, and recovery. A design that meets the target only when every node is available is not equivalent to one sized to meet it after the required failure. Managed services can exchange provider charges for some operating work, while the application team still owns its correctness and recovery decisions. ¶ Finally, relate cost to useful outcomes. Cost per attempted request can look low when many attempts fail or repeat the same operation. Cost per successfully completed business outcome makes those retries and failures visible. Purchase capacity where evidence shows it can increase the required completions. Extra nodes beyond a shared bottleneck can raise the bill while leaving the service's useful throughput almost unchanged. || Same workload and objectives / Include failure and migration headroom / Count network and operating costs / Compare cost per successful outcome''')

put(13, '''A document-processing service receives a PDF. The worker needs the file's bytes, while the application needs to know who owns it, whether the upload finished, and how processing is progressing. These two kinds of data have different jobs. The PDF may remain unchanged while its review status changes several times. ¶ An object store provides operations on named objects containing bytes and associated metadata. The application can place the PDF there and keep its mutable processing record in a database. That record should name a particular object version, along with the owner, expected size, and integrity information needed to recognise the input. Treating completed input versions as immutable means that replacing a file creates another version rather than silently changing the bytes beneath an existing reference. ¶ Follow the handoff between the two stores. First upload the bytes, then verify their identity and completeness, and finally commit the metadata reference that makes them available for processing. The object upload and the database commit are separate operations. If the commit fails, the bytes may exist without a usable document record. If the record is published too early, a worker may see a reference to an incomplete file. Track these states and reconcile abandoned objects instead of treating either storage operation as completion of the whole workflow. ¶ The term object storage describes an interface category. The chosen service and configuration still determine its read, write, versioning, and recovery behaviour. State those guarantees separately from the application's promise that a document is ready. || Upload object bytes / Verify object identity and size / Commit metadata reference / Process only verified complete input

Suppose a user uploads a large scanned report. If the application server receives every byte and forwards it to storage, that transfer occupies the server's network connections and other resources. An alternative is to let the client send the bytes directly to storage while the application coordinates the operation. ¶ The application first checks the user's permission and quota, then creates an upload intent: a record identifying the owner, intended object, and expected limits. It can issue a narrowly scoped authorisation, such as a signed upload URL, for the client to use. Such a URL is a capability: possessing it grants the operation described by its scope and lifetime. Limit it to the intended upload rather than allowing general access to the storage collection. ¶ After the client sends the bytes, the server verifies that the intended object actually completed and satisfies the required size, ownership, and integrity checks. A client message saying “finished” is a request to check completion, not evidence that all the expected bytes were stored. Only after successful verification should the application make a durable processing job ready. ¶ A client can disconnect, abandon an intent, or complete the transfer just before its response is lost. Keep enough state to recognise a repeated completion request. Expire unused intents and clean up unreferenced objects through a process that distinguishes abandoned uploads from uploads still in progress. Direct transfer changes where the bytes travel; the application still owns admission, permissions, and the ready decision. || Create upload intent / Client sends bytes to storage / Server validates completion / Durable job becomes ready

A connection fails near the end of a large upload. Sending the whole file again wastes work that storage has already accepted. A multipart upload divides the file into identified parts and assembles them in a separate completion operation. A resumable upload lets the client continue from confirmed progress, which may be represented by parts or by byte offsets, depending on the protocol. ¶ Consider a session whose manifest expects parts 1, 2, 3, and 4. Storage confirms parts 1, 2, and 4 before the connection fails. On reconnecting, the client checks the session's recorded progress and sends the missing part 3. Retries must use the correct session and part identity so that they replace or confirm the intended part rather than add unrelated data. The manifest specifies the order of assembly; arrival order alone is insufficient. ¶ The client must also keep the source file consistent across attempts. If it sends the first parts of one revision and the final part of a later revision, every part may be present while the assembled file is wrong. Bind the session to the intended source and verify the completed object's size and integrity under the chosen protocol. A provider's entity tag is not universally a hash of the complete file, so do not use it as one without checking its definition. ¶ Confirmed parts are intermediate progress. The application should expose a usable file only after assembly and final verification. Incomplete sessions also need an expiry and cleanup policy because their stored parts consume space even though no completed file appears in the document list. || Upload intent and parts manifest / Confirm parts 1, 2 and 4 / Retry missing part 3 / Complete and verify assembled object

Suppose a file is uploaded and later downloaded. Comparing their lengths can detect missing bytes, but two different files can have the same length. A checksum computes a value from the bytes so that a later computation can help detect changes. The strength of that check depends on the algorithm and the kinds of corruption or interference it is intended to detect. ¶ A suitable cryptographic digest can also be used as a content identity. The application computes the digest of the actual bytes and uses it to name or locate that content. This is content addressing. Under the algorithm's collision assumptions, matching digests provide evidence that the bytes match. The same mechanism can support deduplication: several references can point to one stored copy of identical content. ¶ The check must cover the bytes that were actually received or stored. Recording a digest supplied by the client, without verifying it, records a claim rather than establishing integrity. If the application has an independently expected digest, compare the computed value against it before accepting the input. ¶ Content identity answers which bytes a reference describes. Permission answers whether a caller may access them. A user who knows another tenant's file hash has not gained permission to download that file. Nor does a matching hash show that the file is safe to process. Authorise each reference separately, and consider the scope of deduplication: an endpoint that reveals whether a hash already exists can disclose another tenant's content even without returning the bytes. || Hash actual uploaded bytes / Compare expected digest / Store immutable content identity / Authorize every reference independently

An extractor reads a PDF and records an invoice amount. A week later, the user replaces the PDF. If the earlier result merely names “invoice.pdf,” which bytes did it describe? A mutable filename is insufficient evidence. ¶ Give each binary version a stable identity: F1 for the original bytes and F2 for the replacement. Metadata describes ownership, annotations, classification, processing attempts, and review status while explicitly naming the relevant input. An extraction result should record its file version and the extractor or model version used. Changing review status need not change the original bytes. ¶ When F2 arrives, deliberately update the current-document reference and decide which outputs need recomputation. An F1 output may remain valid history, but it must not silently become an F2 output. A late F1 worker must also be prevented from replacing the current result. This separates “What were the input bytes?” from “What is happening to the document now?” Stable identity makes the relationship explainable; the retention policy determines how long the historical evidence remains available. || Immutable file version F1 / Metadata points to F1 / Extraction output records F1 and model M / New bytes become F2

Consider two documents: D1 contains “blue car,” and D2 contains “blue bike.” To answer a query for car, we could read both documents and inspect their text. With millions of documents, repeating that scan for every query would do a great deal of unnecessary work. ¶ An inverted index organises the lookup in the other direction. For each term, it records the documents containing that term. Here, blue points to D1 and D2, car points to D1, and bike points to D2. Each such collection is a posting list. A query for both blue and car can find their common document, D1. More elaborate search systems also use stored fields, positions, and ranking information, but the term-to-document relationship is the starting point. ¶ Before terms enter the index, an analyser divides text into tokens and may normalise them. For example, its case-handling rule determines whether Blue and blue become the same indexed term. The query must use a compatible interpretation of text. Changing that interpretation can require rebuilding the representation of existing documents. ¶ The index is derived from recoverable source content. Keep document identity, source version, and transformation information so it can be updated or rebuilt. An older extraction must not overwrite the indexed version of a newer file. Search freshness and access also need their own rules: a correctly stored original does not prevent an index from serving an outdated or newly private representation. || D1: blue car / D2: blue bike / blue maps to D1 and D2 / car maps only to D1

The application reports that a PDF was uploaded, but a search for its title finds nothing. Several events may lie between those outcomes. A worker must read the file, extract text, validate the result, perhaps enrich it, and write a searchable representation. An indexing pipeline makes these stages and their progress explicit. ¶ Start a job from a verified source version. Each stage should record the identified input it used and the output or failure it produced. If extraction succeeds but the index write fails, recovery should be able to retry the unfinished step rather than lose the job or assume the whole document is searchable. Malformed files need a bounded retry policy and an actionable review or quarantine state, so one repeatedly failing input does not consume work indefinitely. ¶ A retry should name the same intended document and operation. A stable document ID lets an index update the intended representation rather than insert a second searchable document on every attempt. Version checks are also necessary. A stable ID alone cannot stop an old worker from replacing newer text. A completion must still match the current input version and authorised attempt before its result is accepted. ¶ Finally, distinguish storing an indexed representation from publishing it for users. The application should report the source and index version whose required stages completed, and enforce current access policy when serving it. A copied “public” flag can become stale while processing is under way. Stage progress is useful only when the final published result still belongs to the right version and audience. || Source version ready / Extract and validate text / Index with stable document ID / Publish searchable state and version

Suppose the search analyser changes. Existing index generation A was built using the old analyser, so the application builds generation B while A continues serving searches. Reindexing creates a new searchable representation; it does not stop the source documents from changing during the build. ¶ Begin with a defined source snapshot or change position. That boundary tells us which source state the initial copy represents. While the copy runs, retain a supported way to capture later inserts, updates, and deletions. Then apply those changes to B in a way that preserves the intended versions. If a document is copied and later deleted, the deletion must reach B too. Otherwise the new index can bring removed content back into search. ¶ Before switching, establish a catch-up boundary and validate B against the relevant source state. Check identities, versions, contents, access scope, and deletion handling. Equal counts are weak evidence: B can contain one obsolete document and miss one new document while its count still matches A. Also make sure that changes arriving at the moment of the switch have a defined destination and cannot fall between the build and live-ingestion paths. ¶ A serving alias or routing pointer can select B once these conditions hold. Keep A while the rollback plan remains useful, but recognise that an old generation needs appropriate updates if it is to serve current results again. The safe transition consists of the copy, change capture, validation, and handoff together; changing the pointer is only its visible final step. || Build index generation B / Replay changes after snapshot / Validate versions and deletions / Switch serving pointer from A to B

A team wants yesterday's document totals each morning. It can collect a bounded set of records, process them, and publish one report. This is batch processing. Another team wants an alert soon after each document arrives. Its input keeps arriving, so it uses continuous, or streaming, processing. ¶ The distinction concerns how work and progress are organised. A batch has a defined input boundary and can finish. A stream processor handles an ongoing sequence and records how far it has progressed. Streaming can reduce the delay between an arrival and its result, but the delay still includes ingestion, waiting, computation, and publication. Calling a pipeline streaming does not make its output instantaneous. ¶ Ask what happens when input arrives late. A daily batch can be rerun with a corrected input set. A stream may need to revise a previously published total. In both cases, consumers need to know whether a result is provisional and how corrections will be identified. Reprocessing also needs stable input and output identities so that repair does not create duplicate results. ¶ Choose according to the required freshness, input volume, and recovery work. A scheduled batch may be sufficient when hours of delay are acceptable. Streaming may be justified when later results would miss the purpose of the service, but it adds continuously maintained state, checkpoints, and late-event policies. Batch work has its own resource and failure costs, especially when many jobs compete to finish before the same deadline. || Bounded daily batch / Continuous event stream / Late events may revise output / Progress and recovery need explicit state

An analytical report needs invoice totals but does not need every personal field extracted from the PDFs. Where should the pipeline produce its analytical input? ETL and ELT describe two possible orders. ¶ ETL means extract, transform, load. The pipeline extracts source data, transforms it, then loads the result into the target. It might validate currencies and remove unnecessary personal fields before writing an analytical table. ELT means extract, load, transform: extracted data enters the target environment first, and transformations run there. Loading first can support several later analyses, but the target must then govern the broader raw data it receives. ¶ Choose the placement by processing capabilities, privacy requirements, and query needs. Both arrangements require versioned transformations, input and output checks, and lineage. If a currency conversion rule changes, the team must identify which outputs used the earlier rule and which inputs remain available for repair. The acronym describes step order; the surrounding contracts establish quality and repeatability. || Extract source / Transform before load: ETL / Load before transform: ELT / Version and govern both paths

A document's title changes in the source database. Search and analytics need the change too. Repeatedly scanning every source row can spend substantial work rediscovering records that did not change. Change Data Capture, or CDC, uses a supported mechanism to make source changes available to downstream consumers, commonly from the database's record of committed changes. ¶ A new destination usually needs both existing data and subsequent changes. These must meet at a defined boundary. Imagine copying a row before an edit and beginning the change stream only after that edit. If the edit lies between those two observations, the destination can miss it. A snapshot and stream therefore need a coordinated handoff using the source mechanism's supported position or protocol. ¶ Preserve source identities and enough ordering, version, and schema information to apply changes correctly. A repeated event should not create another business record, and an older update should not undo a newer one. Include deletes and schema changes in the plan. Record durable progress and monitor how far the destination is behind; receiving changes is different from making them visible to queries. ¶ The source may commit several row changes in one transaction while the destination applies them separately. A destination query could then see only part of that transaction unless the application or connector supplies the required grouping. CDC transports changes. The destination's application and serving rules determine what readers can observe. || Source snapshot position recorded / Committed changes stream / Destination applies stable versions / Handoff prevents gaps and duplicate effects

A document service retains PDFs, extracted text, and analytical tables. A worker needs one exact PDF; an analyst scans a year's validated invoice totals. These operations need different representations of the same underlying information. ¶ A data lake retains datasets in flexible forms, often using object storage. A warehouse provides organised analytical structures and querying facilities. Product boundaries overlap, so compare supported operations rather than assigning capabilities from the label. Separate raw source versions from curated outputs whose fields and meanings have been checked. Catalog information should identify each dataset's owner, schema, transformation, and update state. Files alone do not tell a reader whether two datasets use compatible units or versions. ¶ Choose file formats and partitions for actual queries and recovery tasks. Time partitions can reduce unrelated reads for a monthly report, while doing little for a lookup across one user's scattered records. Retained raw inputs can enable replay, but they also preserve sensitive information. Raw and curated datasets alike need a purpose, access policy, lineage, and retention rule. || Raw versioned dataset / Validated curated transformation / Analytical serving layout / Catalog and retention cover every stage

An event records a document arriving at 10:00, but a processor receives it at 10:03. The source occurrence has an event time of 10:00. The processor handles it at a processing time of 10:03. These times answer different questions, and the reported event time also depends on the source's clock and timestamp rules. ¶ Suppose a report groups arrivals into one-minute event-time windows. This event belongs to the interval from 10:00 up to 10:01 even though it reaches the processor later. If that interval's count has already been published, the processor needs a policy: revise the count, emit a correction, or treat the event as too late for that result. Consumers must know what “final” means under the selected policy. ¶ Stream processors maintain state such as counts per tenant and window. A watermark represents an estimate of progress through event time under specified assumptions about delay. It can help decide when to publish or close a window, but it cannot prove that no older event will ever arrive. Define allowed lateness and the treatment of events outside that allowance rather than treating the watermark as a statement about every possible future input. ¶ Recovery needs both processing state and input progress. A checkpoint records information used to resume after failure. If a processor restores an earlier count and rereads input, its publication rules must handle repetition. Saving a checkpoint does not by itself make an external write or notification part of the same atomic operation. State which results can be repeated, corrected, or recomputed after restart. || Event occurs at 10:00 / Processor receives at 10:03 / Window policy handles late data / Checkpoint restores processing state

A reviewer disputes an extracted invoice amount. The current PDF may differ from the one used earlier, and the current model may differ too. We need evidence about the particular output being examined. ¶ Provenance records where a result came from. This field might name file F version 2, page 3 or a specific region, extractor M version 5, relevant parameters, and any review decision. Stable references should locate the evidence actually used. Lineage connects artifacts through transformations: the PDF feeds an extraction run, its fields feed a reviewed dataset, and that dataset feeds search and a report. These links also identify which downstream outputs may need correction. ¶ Such records support investigation without establishing that the value is correct. A recorded model version does not guarantee an identical rerun, especially for nondeterministic execution. Preserve the evidence the intended investigation needs and state reproducibility limits. Provenance records also need a lifecycle: copying personal text into a permanent audit record can undermine deletion of the original. || File F version 2 / Extractor M version 5 / Field derived from page 3 / Human review and downstream output linked

An extracted record contains an amount and a currency, and both have the expected data types. It passes a structural schema check. Yet the number 100 might mean 100 dollars in one version and 100 cents in another. The fields are present, but the interpretation is wrong. ¶ Schema validation checks the supported shape of a record, such as required fields, types, and schema version. Semantic validation checks meaning: units, permitted ranges, relationships, source identity, and business rules. Completeness matters as well. A dataset with valid individual records can still be missing half the expected documents. Compare useful invariants and source versions alongside row counts. ¶ When an input fails validation, quarantine it with a clear reason and enough source evidence to investigate. Quarantine is a controlled state for repair or review, not an instruction to retry the same impossible transformation forever. Once the cause is corrected, replay applies the repaired transformation to the identified inputs. Keep the transform version visible so the new output is distinguishable from the old one. ¶ Replay must preserve effect identity. Reprocessing one field should update or replace the intended result rather than send another payment, email, or other external action. It must also avoid publishing obsolete work. The worked Workflow example rejects epoch 7 when epoch 8 owns the attempt, and rejects file-version-1 when file-version-2 is required. Only the current combination is accepted. In a real store, checking those conditions and publishing the result must be atomic; the small example demonstrates the predicate, not a complete durable pipeline. || Validate input schema / Check semantic invariant / Quarantine invalid record / Replay repaired version without duplicate effect

A user deletes a PDF and can no longer download it. However, a search still returns its extracted text, and a thumbnail remains visible. Deleting the original bytes has removed one artifact while leaving others that contain the same information. ¶ A lifecycle policy states how long data is needed and what happens as it expires, is archived, or is deleted. Follow the document through metadata, extracted fields, previews, search entries, caches, logs, and recoverable copies. Identify which policy applies to each. Keep references that let the deletion workflow find the relevant artifacts without repeatedly scanning all stored files. ¶ Record a durable deletion intent and the progress of its required steps. Failed removals can then be retried. Also consider work already in flight: a worker that read the PDF before deletion may finish afterwards and attempt to publish fresh extracted text. Completion checks must reject work for a deleted document. A minimal deletion marker may be needed to stop old events or replay from recreating it; that marker should retain only the information its purpose requires. ¶ Recovery can reopen the same problem. An older backup may contain a document that was deleted after the backup was taken. Before restored data is exposed, apply the relevant deletion decisions and reconcile its derived copies. Retention limits, recoverable copies, and deletion history must be designed together, so restoring service does not silently restore content that should remain removed. || Deletion intent recorded / Raw and derived artifacts removed / Index and caches updated / Recovery reapplies deletion decisions

Follow one document through the service. Its upload produces a verified binary version. A durable job refers to that version. Extraction produces identified fields, review may correct them, and indexing produces a searchable representation. The user experiences one service even though each step has its own state and can fail separately. ¶ For each handoff, ask what proves that the input is ready, who owns the next step, and what identity a retry must reuse. Record the source and transformation versions. Preserve deterministic transformations where appropriate, and record the relevant configuration and evidence when a transformation cannot be reproduced exactly. A worker should publish only while its input version and attempt remain current; a deleted or replaced document must not be revived by late completion. ¶ Then trace failure at the connections. Bytes may exist before metadata commits. A job may complete just before its reply disappears. A document may change while reindexing runs. Each case needs durable progress, validation, and a recovery rule that reconciles the resulting states. Retain inputs needed for rebuilding within their lifecycle policy, rather than assuming that either infinite history or immediate deletion suits every artifact. ¶ Finally, observe the outcome users need. Queue throughput alone cannot show that documents are searchable, that the oldest accepted job is advancing, or that every result belongs to the correct tenant and source version. Track progress and unresolved failures across stages. A data-intensive application's correctness comes from the connected contracts governing its data, transformations, and effects, as well as the individual storage and messaging components. || Authoritative versioned input / Validated deterministic transformations / Durable processing and effect identities / Reconciled searchable authorized output''')
